{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\nThe `sweep (windows)` job 135597358757 (run 44534782876, main at fa383b1d195, 2026-11-19) failed `tests/unit/test_fixture_env_repr_security.py::test_fixture_failure_does_not_render_unrelated_ambient_secret[tests.scripts.test_observability_exit-healthy_env-None]` at line 71 (`assert result.returncode == 1`). The child pytest session ended with `ERROR collecting test session`: `FileNotFoundError: [WinError 2]` from `Path.lstat()` on `C:\\Users\\runneradmin\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\rebar-ticket-view-534ea34f-s5jdm9m4`. That is a directory in the shared system temp dir, apparently created and then removed by another xdist worker's pinned-ticket-view while the child was enumerating temp. The same test passed in the sweep of run 44526811291 (job 135569438854, main at f8e9ea0e61f) one hour earlier. It is not in any deselect argfile. It recurred in run 44560626779 (job 135688612206, main at 91322a91e9e) on `test_short_traceback_is_a_non_rendering_control[...signals_gerrit_config...]`.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed)\nThe child runs with cwd = the repo (`D:\\a\\rebar\\rebar`) while its test file lives under pytest's tmp_path on `C:` (`%TEMP%\\pytest-of-runneradmin\\...`).\n1. pytest 9.1.1 `_pytest/config/findpaths.py:313-330` (`determine_setup`): with no ini file, rootdir = `get_common_ancestor(invocation_dir, [invocation_dir, ancestor])`. Its `commonpath` (`_pytest/pathlib.py:998-1007`) catches the `ValueError` that `ntpath.commonpath` raises for paths on different drives (\"Paths don't have the same drive\") and returns `None`, so the common ancestor stays the cwd. `D:\\a\\rebar\\rebar` is not a filesystem root, so rootdir and confcutdir become the repo. On POSIX the same call returns `/`, which is a filesystem root, so rootdir falls back to the test file's own directory. That is why macOS never scans temp.\n2. `_pytest/main.py:920-928` (`Session.collect`): every ancestor of the argument that `_is_in_confcutdir` accepts is collected level by level. No `C:` path is an ancestor of a `D:` confcutdir, so collection starts at `C:\\` and runs `Dir.collect` (a scandir) on `C:\\Users\\...\\Temp`.\n3. `_pytest/main.py:986-994`: on `win32`, each scanned entry that does not equal the next path part goes through `samefile_nofollow(node.path, matchparts[0])` (`_pytest/pathlib.py:1049-1054`), which calls `p1.lstat()`. A `rebar-ticket-view-*` sibling removed by another worker between the scandir and the lstat raises `FileNotFoundError`. That is the frame that enumerates `%TEMP%`.\n\nReproduced on macOS (probe in session scratch): a child whose cwd and test file share a non-root ancestor, with `_pytest.main` seeing `win32` and a sibling removed after its parent's scan, gives `ERROR collecting test session` / `FileNotFoundError` and rc 2. Each control that drops 1 of the 3 conditions (win32 matching, the removal, or the non-root shared rootdir) passes.\n\n## Approach\nFix the class in the single sanctioned launcher, `tests/_nested_pytest.py::run_nested_pytest`, which every nested child goes through (a guard in `tests/unit/test_nested_pytest_uniqueness.py` enforces that). When the caller's absolute path arguments (the part before any `::`) all lie outside the child's cwd, and the caller passed none of `--rootdir`, `-c`, `--config-file` or `--confcutdir`, the helper adds `--rootdir <common directory of those arguments>`. This pins the outcome POSIX already gets: rootdir and confcutdir become the test file's own directory, so collection never lists the shared system temp. Path arguments inside the cwd (repo-relative test files) get no new option, so their rootdir, ini file and conftest loading are unchanged. The decision lives in a pure function that takes a `pathmod` argument (default `os.path`), the same injection style `_child_env` uses with `os_name`, so the Windows path logic is unit-testable on any OS. No config key, env var or flag is added.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/_nested_pytest.py`: add the rootdir decision function and append its result in `run_nested_pytest`.\n- `tests/unit/test_nested_pytest_helper.py`: regression tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Pure function with `ntpath`: cwd `D:\\a\\rebar\\rebar` and a `C:\\...\\Temp\\...\\test_render_fixture.py` argument give `--rootdir` set to that file's directory (the failing Windows case). A relative argument, or an absolute argument under cwd, gives nothing. A caller-supplied `--rootdir`/`-c`/`--confcutdir` gives nothing. Absolute arguments on 2 different drives give nothing instead of raising.\n- End to end on any OS: a child whose cwd and test file share a non-root ancestor that holds a `conftest.py` raising at import. Before the fix the child loads that ancestor conftest (rootdir is the shared ancestor) and errors. After the fix the child passes, which proves collection no longer walks above the test file's directory.\n- Existing helper tests stay green (`test_the_call_contract_reaches_subprocess_run` passes no path argument, so its exact command is unchanged).\n\n## Portability\nThe decision uses `os.path`/`ntpath` string functions only: no fds, no signals, no `/tmp`, and a cross-drive `ValueError` is caught. On POSIX, where commonpath already reached `/`, it pins the rootdir pytest already picked, so behavior there is unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Root cause identified with the frame that enumerates the temp dir.\n- [ ] The enumeration tolerates entries removed concurrently, or the child no longer shares the system temp dir, with a regression test that does not need Windows.\n- [ ] The test passes in a `sweep (windows)` run on main after the fix (run id recorded in a comment)."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795110400417584709,"uuid":"adcfcfb0-9360-494b-b09a-6e7008be0c10"}